---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Donation processing software for nonprofits"
  description="Donorbox vs Zeffy vs Givebutter and more — what online donation platforms really cost after platform and processing fees, and which fits your shop."
  section="donation-processing">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Donation processing' }]} />
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">Category guide</p>
      <h1>Donation processing software</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Donation platforms take the money: forms, recurring gifts, and the fees
        that come out of every gift. This is the one category where genuinely
        free options (Zeffy, Givebutter) compete head-on with paid ones
        (Donorbox, GiveWP, 4aGoodCause) — so the real comparison is the fee math,
        not the feature list.
      </p>

      <h2>The fee math, plainly</h2>
      <p>
        Three models compete. <strong>Truly free:</strong> Zeffy charges the
        nonprofit nothing — no platform or processing fee — and asks donors for
        an optional tip instead. <strong>Tip-or-fee:</strong> Givebutter is free
        with donor tips on (0% platform fee) or a flat 3% with tips off, plus
        standard processing (~2.9% + 30¢). <strong>Platform fee:</strong>
        Donorbox starts free with a ~2.95% platform fee on top of processing, and
        a Pro plan (~$150/mo) lowers it. On $100k of online gifts, those models
        can differ by $3,000–$6,000 a year — run your own volume through the
        percentages before choosing.
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        If every fee dollar matters and your needs are forms-and-campaigns —
        take Zeffy and keep 100%. If you run events, auctions, and peer-to-peer
        and want one modern platform, Givebutter's breadth is the draw. If you
        need deeper recurring-gift tooling, integrations, and a mature ecosystem
        — the things that matter as volume grows — that's where
        <strong>Donorbox</strong> earns its percentage, and where WordPress shops
        reach for <strong>GiveWP</strong> instead. Flat-fee holdout
        <strong>4aGoodCause</strong> (from $99/mo, no transaction fees) suits
        shops that want predictable costs.
      </p>

      <h2>The one mistake to avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Don't judge by the platform fee alone. The donor-facing experience moves
        conversion more than a point of fees — a clunky checkout costs you more
        gifts than a 2% fee ever will. Test the actual giving flow on your phone
        before you commit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="donation-processing" />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Processing stays free in <a href="/stacks/under-250k/">the under-$250k
        stack</a> and <a href="/stacks/250k-1m/">the $250k–$1M stack</a>; paid
        tooling enters at <a href="/stacks/1m-5m/">$1M–$5M</a>.
      </p>
      <AffiliateDisclosure />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
